<!-- SECTION 1: Introduction -->
<section id="cot-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Cotangent Ratio Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A cotangent ratio calculator answers one question: for an acute angle in a right triangle, how does the side next to the angle compare with the side across from it? Enter the two side lengths, and the tool divides the adjacent side by the opposite side, returning <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=b/a]]')} />. With an adjacent side of 4 cm and an opposite side of 3 cm, the calculation is <span set:html={rich('[[4/3\\approx1.3333]]')} />: the adjacent side is roughly 1.3333 times as long as the opposite side. That decimal is the complete answer. It carries no units, since centimetres cancel in the division, and it is not an angle in degrees. The hypotenuse never enters this calculation; the two perpendicular legs are sufficient. Cotangent is also the reciprocal of tangent, which is why many textbooks introduce it as the flipped form of <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)]]')} /></p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Understanding the right triangle -->
<section id="cot-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Adjacent Side and Opposite Side</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the angle between two perpendicular lines, like a wall meeting a level floor. The hypotenuse sits opposite that right angle and is always the longest side. The other two angles share the remaining 90 degrees, so both are acute. The two legs meet at the right angle, perpendicular to each other, and it is their relationship that cotangent describes.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the leg that touches the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> without being the hypotenuse. In practical problems it often plays the role of the horizontal run: ground distance from a ladder's base, the horizontal projection of a roof, or the horizontal component of a force. It goes in the numerator of the cotangent formula. Which leg counts as adjacent depends on the chosen reference angle, so switching from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> swaps the adjacent and opposite labels.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> sits directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and never touches its vertex. In practical problems it often plays the role of the vertical rise: the height of a wall, an elevation difference, or the vertical component of a measurement. It goes in the denominator of the cotangent formula, which means making it larger shrinks the ratio and making it smaller grows the ratio.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the side opposite the right angle: in any diagram it faces the small square marking the <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> corner, and it is strictly longer than either leg. It plays no role in the basic cotangent ratio, which is why the calculator never asks for it. It becomes relevant when sine, cosine, secant, or cosecant enter the work, or when the Pythagorean theorem is used to verify that the two legs form a genuine right triangle.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('cotangent-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle with angle A bottom-left, gold adjacent side b, green opposite side a, cot(A)=b/a" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Angle A sits at the bottom-left; cotangent uses the gold adjacent side b and the green opposite side a, not the hypotenuse c.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: What is cotangent -->
<section id="cot-whatis" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Cotangent Ratio</h2>
  <p>Cotangent compares the length next to the reference angle with the length across from it:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\cot(A)=\\frac{b}{a}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)]]')} /> is the cotangent of angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the adjacent side, and <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the opposite side: how many times the opposite side fits into the adjacent side.</p>

  <h3>Cotangent as the Reciprocal of Tangent</h3>
  <p>Tangent divides the opposite side by the adjacent side, <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} />. Taking the reciprocal flips that fraction, <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\tan(A)=b/a]]')} />, which is exactly the cotangent formula:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=\\frac{1}{\\tan(A)}=\\frac{b}{a}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />, the reciprocal is <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1/0.75=4/3\\approx1.3333]]')} />: the same triangle, described from the opposite direction. This distinction matters because <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan^{-1}]]')} /> denotes the inverse tangent function, while <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\tan(A)]]')} /> is the reciprocal tangent: related ideas, different meanings, different answers.</p>

  <h3>Understanding Cotangent as Run Divided by Rise</h3>
  <p>When angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is measured from the horizontal, the adjacent side can represent the horizontal run and the opposite side the vertical rise. Then <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=\\frac{\\text{Horizontal run}}{\\text{Vertical rise}}]]')} />. Tangent gives the same relationship flipped, rise divided by run. This run-over-rise reading makes cotangent the natural ratio for questions like "how much horizontal distance covers how much height?" and it depends on the chosen reference angle and the geometry of the situation.</p>

  <h3>What Does a Cotangent Value Mean?</h3>
  <p>A cotangent greater than 1 means the adjacent side is longer than the opposite side, and the reference angle is less than <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />. A cotangent equal to 1 means the two legs are equal and angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is exactly <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />. A cotangent between 0 and 1 means the opposite side is longer than the adjacent side, and the reference angle lies between <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Why Cotangent Can Be Greater Than 1</h3>
  <p>Unlike sine and cosine, cotangent has no 0-to-1 ceiling because the hypotenuse never enters the division. For <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=8/2=4]]')} />, the adjacent side is simply four times the opposite side, and the reference angle is a relatively small acute angle. Any positive finite value is possible.</p>

  <h3>Why Cotangent Can Be Less Than 1</h3>
  <p>For <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=3/6=0.5]]')} />, the adjacent side is half the opposite side, so the reference angle is a relatively large acute angle. The ratio drops below 1 without anything going wrong: it is simply the signature of a taller, narrower triangle.</p>

  <h3>Why Cotangent Is a Dimensionless Ratio</h3>
  <p>Dividing one length by another cancels the units: <span set:html={rich('[[4\\text{ m}/3\\text{ m}=4/3]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[400\\text{ cm}/300\\text{ cm}=4/3]]')} /> as well. The number describes the shape of the triangle, not a measurement with physical units, so write the result as a plain number, never with metres, centimetres, or degrees attached.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('cotangent-reciprocal.svg')} alt="Three stages: 3-4-5 triangle labeled, tangent tan(A)=3/4=0.75, cotangent cot(A)=4/3=1.3333, the reciprocal relationship" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Three stages: label the 3-4-5 triangle, compute tangent tan(A) = 3/4, then flip the fraction to get cot(A) = 4/3.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: Formula derivation -->
<section id="cot-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Cotangent Formula Works</h2>
  <p>Start from the definition: cotangent is the adjacent side divided by the opposite side. In this page's notation:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=\\frac{b}{a}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The adjacent side is the numerator because it is the length being compared; the opposite side is the denominator because it is the reference length, the "how many of these fit into that" unit. Swapping them gives the tangent ratio instead.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=b/a]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Substitute the measurements. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Divide. <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 4: Interpret the answer. The adjacent side is about 1.3333 times the opposite side.</p>
  <p>Step 5: Verify with the tangent ratio. <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[1/0.75\\approx1.3333]]')} />. Both paths land on the same number, which is exactly how reciprocal ratios behave.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: Calculator walkthrough -->
<section id="cot-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Cotangent Ratio Calculator</h2>
  <p>The walkthrough below matches the actual interface on this page.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />,</strong> since the adjacent and opposite sides are defined relative to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />:</strong> the leg touching angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> that is not the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />:</strong> the leg directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, the one that never touches its vertex.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure or obtain both side lengths</strong> in the same unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the adjacent-side length</strong> in the first input field.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the opposite-side length</strong> in the second input field.</li>
    <li><strong>Select matching units with the unit selectors</strong> so the division compares like with like.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm both measurements are positive;</strong> zero or negative lengths cannot form a real triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate cot(A)</strong> to divide the adjacent side by the opposite side.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the cotangent ratio:</strong> the adjacent side expressed as a multiple of the opposite side.</li>
    <li><strong>Interpret the result:</strong> greater than 1 means a longer adjacent side, equal to 1 means equal legs, less than 1 means a longer opposite side.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset</strong> to clear the inputs and start over.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator asks for no hypotenuse and no angle in degrees: two positive side lengths are the complete input, and the result is always a positive, dimensionless number.</p>
</section>

