<section id="cot-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Calculating Cotangent</h2>
  <p>Each example follows the same pattern: write the formula, substitute, divide, interpret.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The adjacent side is about 1.3333 times the opposite side; the hypotenuse sits out of the calculation entirely.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=12/5]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=2.4}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The ratio is greater than 1 because the adjacent side is the longer leg; it tells us the adjacent side is 2.4 times the opposite side, not how large the triangle is.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: An Isosceles Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=6\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=6\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=6\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=6/6]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=1}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Equal legs give a ratio of exactly 1, which is the signature of a <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /> reference angle.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A Triangle With a Smaller Adjacent Side</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=6\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\text{ m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=6/8]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=0.75}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is less than 1 because the opposite side is the longer leg, so the reference angle is greater than <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /></p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7: Twelve practical examples -->
<section id="cot-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Using the Cotangent Ratio</h2>
  <p>Cotangent is a mathematical ratio, not a physical quantity with units. Its practical value lies in comparing horizontal run with vertical rise, checking triangle geometry, and using formulas that specifically call for adjacent divided by opposite. Each example assumes a simplified right triangle, and none of these ratios alone settles questions of safety, strength, or code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Finding a Ladder's Run-to-Rise Ratio</h3>
  <p>A ladder rests against a vertical wall. Its base is 3 m from the wall, and it reaches 4 m up the wall. The horizontal distance is the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b=3\\text{ m}]]')} />, the vertical reach the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a=4\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=3/4]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=0.75}]]')} />: the horizontal distance is 75 percent of the vertical height. The ratio describes the ladder's geometry; it does not determine whether the ladder is safely positioned.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Calculating a Roof's Run-to-Rise Ratio</h3>
  <p>A roof section has a horizontal run of 4 m and a vertical rise of 3 m. Here <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} />: the horizontal run is about 1.3333 times the vertical rise. This ratio is not a complete structural or roofing-code assessment.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Evaluating a Ramp's Cotangent Ratio</h3>
  <p>A simplified ramp has a horizontal run of 9 m and a vertical rise of 0.75 m. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=9\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=0.75\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=9/0.75]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=12}]]')} />. The horizontal run is 12 times the vertical rise, the signature of a gentle slope. The ratio alone does not determine accessibility or building-code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: A Surveying Measurement</h3>
  <p>A simplified surveying triangle has a horizontal distance of 40 m and a vertical elevation difference of 30 m. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=40\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=30\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=40/30]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} />. The ratio compares the horizontal separation with the vertical difference directly. Actual surveying can require additional measurements, corrections, and instrument procedures.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: A Guy Wire Supporting a Pole</h3>
  <p>A guy wire connects a pole to a ground anchor. The anchor is 12 m horizontally from the pole, and the attachment point is 5 m above the ground. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=12/5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=2.4}]]')} />. The horizontal distance is 2.4 times the vertical separation. This ratio does not establish wire tension, anchor strength, or structural safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Understanding a Staircase's Run-to-Rise Ratio</h3>
  <p>A simplified staircase has a horizontal run of 4 m and a vertical rise of 3 m. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} />. The horizontal run is about 1.3333 times the vertical rise. The example describes an idealized diagonal and does not verify staircase dimensions or code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: A Crane Boom's Horizontal and Vertical Components</h3>
  <p>A simplified crane boom forms a right triangle with a horizontal projection of 6 m and a vertical rise of 8 m. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=6\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=6/8]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=0.75}]]')} />. The horizontal projection is 75 percent of the vertical component. The ratio does not establish lifting capacity or operating safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: A Straight Aircraft Flight Segment</h3>
  <p>For a simplified flight segment, an aircraft travels along a straight path with a horizontal displacement of 4 km and a vertical gain of 3 km. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ km}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ km}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} />. The horizontal displacement is about 1.3333 times the vertical gain. This is an idealized geometric model, not a complete flight-navigation calculation.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: A Diagonal Mechanical Brace</h3>
  <p>A diagonal brace has a horizontal offset of 1.2 m and a vertical rise of 1.6 m. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=1.2\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.6\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1.2/1.6]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=0.75}]]')} />. The horizontal offset is 75 percent of the vertical rise. The ratio does not determine the brace's strength or suitability.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: A Straight Hiking Section</h3>
  <p>A simplified straight section of a hiking route has a horizontal displacement of 80 m and a vertical gain of 60 m. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=80\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=60\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=80/60]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} />. The horizontal displacement is about 1.3333 times the elevation gain. Actual trails can have curves and changing gradients.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: An Awning Support</h3>
  <p>A diagonal awning support has a horizontal projection of 2.4 m and a vertical rise of 1.8 m. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=2.4\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.8\\text{ m}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=2.4/1.8]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333}]]')} />. The ratio describes the horizontal projection relative to the vertical rise. This calculation does not establish structural suitability.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: A Cable Spanning Two Different Heights</h3>
