<!-- SECTION 8: Cotangent and angle size -->
<section id="cot-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Relationship Between Cotangent and Angle Size</h2>
  <p>Cotangent falls as the acute angle grows. A small angle keeps the adjacent side much longer than the opposite side, so the ratio is large; as angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> opens toward <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the opposite side takes over and the ratio drops toward 0. The verified reference values below show the pattern:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Angle A</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Adjacent side b</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Opposite side a</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Cotangent ratio</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">30 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sqrt(3)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 1.7321</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">45 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5">1</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">60 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sqrt(3)</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 0.5774</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">75 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Approximately 3.7321</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 0.2679</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The table measurements are illustrative: the ratios, not the absolute lengths, determine the angles. The ratio grows without bound as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} /> and approaches 0 as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, staying positive and finite for every valid triangle.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('cotangent-angle-change.svg')} alt="Three triangles at 30, 45, 60 degrees with cotangent values 1.7321, 1, 0.5774; cotangent falls as the angle grows" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">As angle A grows, the opposite side becomes a larger share of the adjacent side, so cot(A) falls.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>The calculator takes no angle input; these reference values serve as orientation and check values when verifying a result.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 9: Finding an angle from cotangent -->
<section id="cot-recover" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find an Angle When Cotangent Is Known</h2>
  <p>The cotangent ratio is not itself an angle. When the ratio is known and the angle is required, inverse trigonometry bridges the gap. For a positive cotangent ratio in a right triangle, take the reciprocal to recover the tangent ratio, then apply inverse tangent:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\cot(A)}\\right)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Equivalently, since <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A=\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)}]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Worked Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3]]')} />: the reciprocal is <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(3/4)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{A\\approx36.87^\\circ}]]')} />. Set the scientific calculator to degrees mode before pressing the inverse tangent key. This is a separate angle calculation; the cotangent ratio calculator on this page takes side lengths and returns the ratio, never the angle.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 10: Common mistakes -->
<section id="cot-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Cotangent</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Reversing the formula to <span set:html={rich('[[a/b]]')} /> instead of <span set:html={rich('[[b/a]]')} />.</strong> This computes tangent. Fix: put the adjacent side on top.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing cotangent with tangent.</strong> Reporting 0.75 for a 3-4-5 triangle. Fix: tangent is opposite over adjacent; cotangent is the flipped fraction.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing cotangent with cosine.</strong> Using <span set:html={rich('[[b/c]]')} />. Fix: cosine needs the hypotenuse; cotangent never touches it.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the hypotenuse instead of one of the two legs.</strong> Computing <span set:html={rich('[[b/c]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[c/a]]')} />. Fix: the denominator is the opposite leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing up the adjacent and opposite sides.</strong> Choosing the wrong leg. Fix: trace from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />: the leg touching it is adjacent, the one across from it is opposite.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting that the side labels depend on the reference angle.</strong> Using labels drawn for angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. Fix: relabel whenever the reference angle changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming cotangent must be between 0 and 1.</strong> Treating 2.4 or 12 as errors. Fix: those values are fine; only sine and cosine are capped at 1.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming a cotangent of 1 means an invalid triangle.</strong> Fix: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1]]')} /> simply means equal legs and a <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} /> angle, a perfectly valid triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting the ratio with metres or centimetres.</strong> Writing 1.3333 m. Fix: the units already cancelled, so report a plain number.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering zero or negative side lengths.</strong> Division by zero or a meaningless negative ratio. Fix: use positive lengths only.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing measurement units.</strong> Metres divided by centimetres skew the ratio by 100. Fix: convert both sides to one unit first.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding too early.</strong> Rounding <span set:html={rich('[[4/3]]')} /> to 1.33 before verifying adds avoidable error. Fix: keep extra digits until the final step.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming cotangent is an angle in degrees.</strong> Reading 1.3333 as 1.3333 degrees. Fix: it is a ratio; the angle needs inverse tangent.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing reciprocal tangent with inverse tangent.</strong> Writing <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\tan(A)]]')} /> when <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan^{-1}(a/b)]]')} /> is needed, or the reverse. Fix: reciprocal flips the fraction; inverse recovers the angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the cotangent value determines the triangle's size.</strong> Two very different triangles can share <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1.3333]]')} />. Fix: the ratio fixes the shape, not the scale.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />:</p>
  <p>Incorrect: <span set:html={rich('[[3/4=0.75]]')} />. This is the tangent ratio, not cotangent.</p>
  <p>Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=4/3\\approx1.3333]]')} />. Verify: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1/0.75\\approx1.3333]]')} />, which confirms the reciprocal relationship.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 11: Verification -->
<section id="cot-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Cotangent Result Is Valid</h2>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle requires positive legs, <span set:html={rich('[[a \\gt 0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b \\gt 0]]')} />, so the cotangent result must be a positive, finite number:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cot(A)=\\frac{b}{a} \\gt 0}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both measurements must use compatible units. A result greater than 1, equal to 1, or less than 1 can all be valid; unlike sine and cosine, cotangent is not capped at 1.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Check the Reciprocal of Tangent</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=a/b]]')} />, then confirm <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1/\\tan(A)]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=3/4=0.75]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1/0.75\\approx1.3333]]')} />. A mismatch between the two paths signals a reversed fraction or a mislabeled side.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>If the hypotenuse is known or needed, calculate <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{9+16}=5]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />. This confirms the side lengths form a genuine right triangle. The hypotenuse is a verification value here, not part of the primary cotangent formula.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Check the Angle Relationship</h3>
  <p>For a known cotangent ratio, recover the angle with <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\tan^{-1}(a/b)]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Because the adjacent side is longer than the opposite side, the angle should land below <span set:html={rich('[[45^\\circ]]')} />; if it does not, the side labels are probably swapped. Small differences may arise from rounding.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 12: Choosing a ratio -->
<section id="cot-vs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Cotangent vs. Tangent, Sine, and Cosine</h2>
  <p>Each ratio compares a different pair of sides, using the same reference angle and side labels:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Ratio</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">What it compares</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Sine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sin(A) = a/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cos(A) = b/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Tangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">tan(A) = a/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cotangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cot(A) = b/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to opposite side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Secant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sec(A) = c/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosecant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">csc(A) = c/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to opposite side</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Cotangent and tangent are the reciprocal pair: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)=1/\\tan(A)]]')} />. If <span set:html={rich('[[\\tan(A)=0.75]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[\\cot(A)\\approx1.3333]]')} />. Reach for cotangent when a formula calls for adjacent divided by opposite, when you are working run-to-rise relationships, or when only the two legs are known; reach for tangent for rise-to-run, sine for the opposite side against the hypotenuse, and cosine for the adjacent side against the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>For the neighbouring ratios, see the <a href="/sine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Sine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/cosine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cosine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/tangent-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Tangent Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/secant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Secant Ratio Calculator</a>, and the <a href="/cosecant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cosecant Ratio Calculator</a>.</p>
</section>

